---
# IS EVERY CINEMA STILL BEING SCRAPED? -- the one question none of the other application alerts
# can answer.
#
# Every other scrape signal measures the scrapes that HAPPENED. `kinowo_worker_http_total
# {phase="scrape"}`, the handler durations, the task-queue depth: all of them are counters a
# working scrape increments. A cinema that quietly stops being scraped increments nothing, so a
# roster with a dark venue emits exactly the same series as a healthy one, only slightly fewer of
# them. That is not a threshold anybody can set. It is why `CinemaScrapeCensus` exists and why
# these rules read its two gauges rather than any of the traffic counters.
#
# The gauges (worker/src/main/scala/services/metrics/CinemaScrapeCensus.scala, sampled once a
# minute over the in-memory freshness mirror the ScrapeReaper itself schedules from, so the metric
# and the scheduler cannot disagree):
#
#   kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds{country}
#       How long ago the LEAST recently scraped cinema in that country last scraped SUCCESSFULLY.
#       The roster's worst case, not its average.
#   kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_never_scraped{country}
#       How many cinemas carry no successful scrape at all. Counted separately and never folded
#       into the age above, because an unknown age is not a zero one.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT "EXCEEDING EXPECTED TIME" MEANS HERE, AND WHY THE THRESHOLD IS PER COUNTRY
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is no fleet-wide number to compare against. Each country deploys its own sweep cadence in
# `KINOWO_SCRAPE_FRESHNESS_MINUTES` (movies-gitops/worker/overlays/<cc>/patch.yaml), and they
# are an order of magnitude apart -- 60 minutes for PL against 840 for the US, because the US
# roster is ~5,000 Flicks venues behind a 200ms measured pace and PL's is ~300. A single threshold
# would either page PL eleven hours late or page the US constantly. So each country is compared to
# ITS OWN window.
#
# HEALTHY IS A SAWTOOTH RIDING JUST UNDER THAT WINDOW, which is the reason for the 1.5x multiplier
# rather than a comparison against the window itself. Cinemas fall due, get swept, and the maximum
# resets -- so the gauge is SUPPOSED to approach the window from below and drop. DE peaked near
# 9.6h against its 10h window on 2026-08-30 while running comfortably ahead of schedule. A rule
# that fired at 1.0x would fire on healthy operation every cycle; the whole margin is there so that
# only a sweep genuinely failing to keep up says anything.
#
# 1.5x IS WIGGLE ROOM, NOT A MEASUREMENT, and it is worth being honest about that. It is one half-
# window of slack, chosen because it is comfortably outside the sawtooth's normal peak and still
# far inside the shape that has actually bitten: DE's real overrun in July 2026 sat at ~12.8h
# against a 3h window -- more than 4x -- and ran for weeks with every other panel clean. Anything
# in the 1.2-2.0x range would have caught it. Revise this by looking at what the gauge did in the
# week before it fired, not by raising it because it fired.
#
# THE MULTIPLIER AND THE PER-COUNTRY NUMBERS BELOW ARE GENERATED AND ASSERTED by
# `CinemaScrapeStalenessAlertSpec` in the application repository, from the same deploy configs the
# workers actually read. They are written out here as literals because PromQL has no way to reach
# a ConfigMap, and a literal that nothing checks is how the oldest-scrape PANEL came to misstate
# DE's window within an hour of being written. Change a cadence without changing these and that
# spec fails by name.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE `country` LABEL comes from the SCRAPE, not from the application: scrape-kinowo-apps.yaml
# gives each worker's NodePort target a `country` label and drops the app's own as
# `exported_country`. So it is true by construction -- a worker misconfigured onto the wrong
# database still reports under the country whose target was polled, which is the half of that
# failure worth being told about.
groups:
  - name: cinema-scrape
    rules:
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE ROSTER IS AGEING PAST ITS CADENCE.
      #
      # One clause per country, never a grouping, and never a shared constant: PL and UK shared a
      # value for a few hours in July 2026 and the guard written around that coincidence failed on
      # the GROUPING the moment UK moved. Spelling each country out has no such coupling, and an
      # `or` chain still yields one alert instance per breaching country, so a fleet-wide sweep
      # failure pages five times with five names rather than once with none.
      #
      # A country absent from this list is NOT watched. That is the failure mode to look for after
      # onboarding one -- the spec named in this file's header asserts every deployed worker
      # overlay has a clause here, so it cannot happen quietly.
      - alert: CinemaScrapeOldestAgeHigh
        expr: >-
          kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds{country="de"} > 54000
          or kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds{country="es"} > 37800
          or kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds{country="pl"} > 5400
          or kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds{country="uk"} > 37800
          or kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds{country="us"} > 75600
        # THIRTY MINUTES, and it is doing less work than it looks like. Once the oldest cinema is
        # past 1.5x its window the number only climbs -- a failed scrape deliberately does not mark
        # freshness, so nothing resets it until that venue is scraped successfully. The `for:` is
        # therefore not waiting for the condition to persist, it is filtering the one-sample spike
        # a worker restart can leave while the census re-reads the freshness mirror.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s oldest cinema has not scraped in {{ $value | humanizeDuration }}"
          description: >-
            The least recently scraped cinema in "{{ $labels.country }}" is past 1.5x that
            country's KINOWO_SCRAPE_FRESHNESS_MINUTES window, so the sweep is not keeping up with
            its own cadence. Read the shape before reading the number: a line climbing in a
            straight diagonal is ONE cinema failing every attempt (a failed scrape does not mark
            freshness, so it stays stale for ever and drags the maximum with it), while an
            oscillating but high ceiling is the whole sweep running slower than its window -- a
            slow upstream, a pacing change, or a roster that grew. Panel 58 on the application
            dashboard draws it. Check the worker's logs for a venue erroring on every pass, and
            `RealHttpFetch.HostPolicies` for a pace that has been tightened without the cadence
            being widened to match; that pairing is what put DE 4x over its window in July 2026
            with every other panel clean. If the sweep is simply too slow for the window, the
            cadence is the lever, and `WorkerScrapeCadenceConfigSpec` asserts the arithmetic.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # CINEMAS THAT HAVE NEVER SCRAPED AT ALL -- the hole the rule above is structurally blind to.
      #
      # A cinema with no successful scrape carries NO AGE, deliberately: the census counts it here
      # instead of folding an unknown age in as a zero one. Which means the worst possible case --
      # a venue added to the roster and never once fetched -- moves `oldest_age_seconds` not at
      # all. Without this rule the staleness alerting would be silent on exactly the venue that has
      # been dark longest.
      - alert: CinemaScrapeNeverScraped
        expr: kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_never_scraped > 0
        # TWENTY-FOUR HOURS, and it MUST stay longer than the slowest country's full sweep. Every
        # cinema is legitimately never-scraped on a fresh worker until the first sweep reaches it,
        # so a shorter window would page on every US rollout -- 14h cadence, and 1.5x of that is
        # 21h. 24h clears it with room and is still the same day.
        for: 24h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $value }} cinema(s) in {{ $labels.country }} have never been scraped successfully"
          description: >-
            These venues carry no successful scrape at all, so they contribute no age to
            CinemaScrapeOldestAgeHigh and appear in no scrape counter -- they are invisible to
            every other signal. A full day is longer than any country's sweep, so this is not a
            cold start. Most likely a venue was added to the roster with a URL that has never
            resolved, or a client throws before it can record anything. Panel 59 on the application
            dashboard counts them per country; the `cinema_scrapes` archive names which.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE COMPANION, in the shape mongodb.rules uses throughout: a rule that can only fire on a
      # series has to be paired with one that fires on the series' ABSENCE, or the monitoring's own
      # failure looks exactly like health. Both rules above are the quiet kind -- they say nothing
      # when their series is missing, and a worker that has stopped publishing its census gauges
      # would leave them permanently Normal.
      #
      # `absent()` OVER THE WHOLE METRIC, not per country. A per-country `absent()` would need five
      # rules and would fire during any deliberate single-country stop (the 2026-08-02 poland-only
      # month would have paged for four countries for four weeks). This one asks only whether
      # ANYBODY is publishing the census at all, which is a bug in every case.
      - alert: CinemaScrapeCensusAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds)
        # ONE HOUR: longer than a rollout of every worker in the fleet, shorter than PL's window.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No worker is publishing the cinema-scrape census"
          description: >-
            kinowo_worker_cinema_scrape_oldest_age_seconds has no series from any country, so
            CinemaScrapeOldestAgeHigh and CinemaScrapeNeverScraped cannot fire and the roster is
            unwatched. Either every worker is down -- in which case TargetDown in
            monitoring-self.rules is also firing and is the better alert to read -- or
            `CinemaScrapeCensus` has stopped being scheduled, which TargetDown would NOT catch
            because the pod is up and serving /metrics without this gauge in it. AN ABSENT CENSUS
            IS NOT A FRESH ROSTER.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # A VENUE LEFT THE ROSTER AND ITS ROWS DID NOT.
      #
      # `kinowo_worker_retired_venue_side_rows` counts `screenings`/`movie_slots` rows filed under
      # a venue name this country's roster no longer lists (RetiredVenueCensus, hourly, id-only
      # reads). Nothing serves them, and until a cleanup removes them nothing deletes them: after
      # "Kino Etiuda OBK" left the PL roster its 3 films and 8 future showtimes sat there for days,
      # counted as live by every census that reads the side collections raw.
      #
      # FIFTY HOURS, NOT A DAY, because of how the cleanup is built. `RetiredVenueRows.sweep` runs
      # on the daily stranded-row tick and leaves any row written in the last 24h alone (a rolling
      # deploy's OLD pod must not delete rows the NEW pod's roster has just added). So a venue
      # removed in a deploy is legitimately above zero for up to 24h of grace plus up to 24h until
      # the next tick. Fifty hours is that plus two of slack; past it the cleanup did not happen --
      # it refused (an empty roster, or more than 20% of stored venues retired at once), its read
      # failed, or it is not running. It is the watchdog for that cleanup, not a replacement.
      #
      # IT WOULD FIRE TODAY if the rows stayed. Measured against prod on 2026-09-23 with the roster
      # at origin/main: PL holds 6 rows under Kino Etiuda OBK (7 future showtimes) and movie_slots
      # rows for 28 DE/UK venues (a foreign venue's row in a country's own database is as
      # unservable as a retired one), UK 20 under Odeon Cinema Basingstoke, DE 1 under "Studio";
      # ES and US none. The cleanup landed today, so these should step down within two days.
      - alert: RetiredVenueRowsLingering
        expr: max by (country) (kinowo_worker_retired_venue_side_rows) > 0
        for: 50h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} still holds {{ $value }} side row(s) under a venue its roster no longer lists"
          description: >-
            `screenings` or `movie_slots` rows in "{{ $labels.country }}" have been filed under a venue
            name missing from that country's roster for over two days -- longer than the daily
            retired-venue cleanup and its 24h grace allow. Nothing serves them, and that cleanup
            evidently has not removed them (look for its RemovalAudit line or its refusal WARN,
            "Retired-venue rows: ..." in the worker log); every
            census that reads the side collections raw counts their future showtimes
            (`kinowo_worker_retired_venue_future_showtimes`) as live. Name the venues with
            `db.screenings.aggregate([{$group: {_id: {$arrayElemAt: [{$split: ["$slotKey", "␟"]}, 0]}, n: {$sum: 1}}}])`
            against the country's database and compare with its roster. A foreign country's venue
            in the list means rows were written into the wrong database.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # ONE SCREEN, TWO NAMES -- OR ONE VENUE'S FEED UNDER ANOTHER'S.
      #
      # `kinowo_worker_duplicate_venue_pairs` counts roster venue pairs whose upcoming (film,
      # start time) programmes are (nearly) the same (DuplicateVenueCensus, off the 5-minute corpus
      # scan; venues under 5 upcoming showtimes skipped; pairs on `DistinctVenuePairs` cleared),
      # in two scopes. scope="same_city": two venues of one city at 90%+ of the larger programme
      # -- the same screen under two names, which the name-based roster audit cannot see.
      # scope="cross_city": two venues sharing no city, 20+ showtimes each, at 95%+ -- an
      # aggregator feeding one venue's programme under another's name far away (Flicks gave the
      # Syracuse IN Pickwick the Park Ridge IL one's, 216 of 216). The cross-city bar is higher
      # because a chain's template schedule (Odeon) runs near 90% between towns.
      #
      # TWENTY-FOUR HOURS: programmes move with every scrape, and a pair that matches for a day
      # is not a coincidence of one evening's schedule.
      #
      # `last_over_time(...[10m])` BRIDGES A WORKER RESTART. Each pod change leaves the series
      # absent for ~3 minutes (staleness marker, then boot), and one absent evaluation drops a
      # pending alert, so the bare gauge only reached `for: 24h` after a day with no deploy --
      # with ~16 worker restarts on 2026-09-23 and one day-long uptime in the week before it,
      # effectively never. Ten minutes covers the gap and keeps a cleared pair clearing within
      # two corpus scans.
      #
      # Measured against prod on 2026-09-23 it would have fired in five countries; every pair is
      # now either fixed at its source (Koło's Kino nad Wartą repeating Konin's; the Syracuse
      # Pickwick; Braniewo's Baszta repeating Środa's, fixed that morning) or on
      # `DistinctVenuePairs` with its ticketing evidence (chain-mates in both scopes).
      - alert: DuplicateVenueListing
        expr: max by (country, scope) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_duplicate_venue_pairs[10m])) > 0
        for: 24h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $value }} {{ $labels.scope }} venue pair(s) in {{ $labels.country }} have shared their programme for a day"
          description: >-
            Two roster venues in "{{ $labels.country }}" have listed (nearly) the same upcoming films
            at the same start times for 24 hours. same_city (90%+, one city): one screen listed
            twice under two names, or two venues wired to one upstream listing. cross_city (95%+,
            no city in common): one venue's feed listed under another's name. The worker's WARN
            line "duplicate-venue census ({{ $labels.country }}, {{ $labels.scope }})" names the
            pairs. Compare each pair's booking links and sources in CinemaScraperCatalog: one
            upstream programme behind two names is a wiring bug to fix (or a venue with no feed
            of its own to drop); a chain genuinely running one programme in two towns goes on
            `services.cinemas.roster.DistinctVenuePairs` with its reason, which clears it here and
            in the offline roster audit alike.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # A COUNTRY'S SCRAPES ARE FAILING, NOT COMING BACK EMPTY.
      #
      # The share of the last 30 minutes' cinema uptime checks that FAILED (fetch or parse threw),
      # out of every check (failed + succeeded + parsed-but-empty), from the web tier's
      # `kinowo_uptime_recent_*` per-service gauges -- cinema services only: the enrichment
      # sources, hosts, image proxies and the residential proxy have rules of their own.
      #
      # WHY ZEROES ARE IN THE DENOMINATOR AND NOT THE NUMERATOR: an empty listing is what a
      # seasonally closed venue legitimately returns (~120 of ~2,800 services at any moment), so it
      # must never read as a failure. Until 2026-09-23 the reverse was the bug: Cinema1 and several
      # scrapers turned a fetch or parse FAILURE into an empty scrape, so a broken parser looked
      # like a closed cinema, marked its freshness and fired nothing. They now throw; a throw is a
      # failed check here AND, since a failed scrape never marks freshness, a venue failing every
      # attempt still reaches `CinemaScrapeOldestAgeHigh` above. This rule is the other shape: a
      # SHARED code path (a chain client, a proxy, a portal) breaking many venues at once, which
      # the per-country worst-case age only notices hours later, if the venues ever succeed at all.
      #
      # 20% FOR TWO HOURS, measured on 14 days of the gauges (2026-09-09 .. 09-23, 30-min steps):
      # normal p99 is de 0.10, es 0.06, us 0.02, uk 0.02, pl 0.17, and the one PL excursion above
      # 0.2 (0.37 on 09-10 07:58) lasted under an hour. Two real, sustained spikes crossed it:
      # PL at ~0.30 and UK at 0.75-0.95 from the evening of 09-18 to 09-21 17:00 -- three days,
      # alerted by nothing. This would have fired for UK at ~23:45 on 09-18 and PL at ~00:00 on
      # 09-19 (replayed against Prometheus). At least 10 checks in the
      # window, so a country's quiet half-hour cannot make one failure a 50% share.
      - alert: CinemaScrapeFailureShareHigh
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (kinowo_uptime_recent_failures{service!~"IMDb|TMDB|Filmweb|Metacritic|Rotten Tomatoes|Residential proxy|img: .*|[a-z0-9.-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}|.*\\|enrichment"})
            /
            (sum by (country) (
                kinowo_uptime_recent_failures{service!~"IMDb|TMDB|Filmweb|Metacritic|Rotten Tomatoes|Residential proxy|img: .*|[a-z0-9.-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}|.*\\|enrichment"}
              + kinowo_uptime_recent_successes{service!~"IMDb|TMDB|Filmweb|Metacritic|Rotten Tomatoes|Residential proxy|img: .*|[a-z0-9.-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}|.*\\|enrichment"}
              + kinowo_uptime_recent_zeroes{service!~"IMDb|TMDB|Filmweb|Metacritic|Rotten Tomatoes|Residential proxy|img: .*|[a-z0-9.-]+\\.[a-z]{2,}|.*\\|enrichment"}
            ) >= 10)
          ) > 0.2
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of {{ $labels.country }}'s cinema scrape checks have been failing for two hours"
          description: >-
            More than a fifth of the cinema uptime checks in "{{ $labels.country }}" have FAILED
            (fetch or parse threw, not an empty listing) for two hours straight. That is a shared
            code path, not one venue: a chain client, a ticketing portal, a proxy or an upstream
            that changed its markup or started refusing us. Open https://kinowo.net/uptime (or the
            country's own /uptime) and group the red rows by source host -- the one host they share
            is the cause, and its error text is on the row. Empty listings are NOT counted: a
            seasonally closed venue returns nothing and is healthy. A single venue failing every
            attempt shows up in CinemaScrapeOldestAgeHigh instead.
